00:00:02.600 --> 00:00:08.470 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  one of the greatest bottlenecks in the  area of gene therapy is delivery  if you can't deliver a therapeutic  payload into a diseased cell  they sort of have no hope of curing that  cell in any meaningful way  and so in attempting to solve this  problem we are highly inspired by  viruses  which through the process of evolution  have developed elegant methods by which  to infiltrate host cells and one method  used by a certain class of viruses that  infect bacteria  called bacteriophages and here's a  little bacteriophage virus  and so these phages utilize this really  elegant macromolecular syringe device  and here's this kind of syringe  structure  beneath the capsid of the virus and they  use these syringes to  sort of directly puncture their host  membranes  and this device is known as a  contractile injection system this kind  of syringe structure  in a manner similar to the action of a  virus injecting its genome into a host  cell  we are developing contractile injection  systems capable of injecting therapeutic  proteins directly into human cells  and through this project we first  successfully purified a novel  contractile injection system  from a laboratory organism e coli we  next show that the system is capable  injecting large protein cargoes into  cultured eukaryotic cells  finally we're currently reprogramming  the system for injection against  cultured human cells  with the ultimate intention of  developing safe and specific therapeutic  delivery devices  with these contractile injection systems  to enable the next generation of gene  [Music]  therapy

